menu "APRS heap instrumentation"

    config APRS_HEAP_REPORT
        bool "Log a periodic heap line"
        default y
        help
            Emit one line per period, from the APRS service's 1 Hz tick, with
            the free heap, the largest free block and the sum of the per-heap
            all-time minimums.

            These three figures are what describe the allocator on the healthy
            path. Every other heap number this firmware logs is printed after
            something has already failed, which says what the heap looked like
            once the damage was done and nothing about how it got there. A
            watermark that fell overnight leaves no trace without this line.

            It costs three allocator queries per period and no memory, so it
            is meant to stay on in a normal build.

    config APRS_HEAP_REPORT_PERIOD_S
        int "Seconds between heap lines"
        depends on APRS_HEAP_REPORT
        range 10 3600
        default 10
        help
            The period has to be shorter than the events it is meant to catch,
            and on this firmware those are short: a TLS handshake, an APRS-IS
            reconnect or a settings save each peak and recover within a few
            seconds. A sampler slower than that is structurally blind to them -
            it records the heap before and after, never during, and every line
            it prints is consistent with a peak that never happened.

            Ten seconds does not resolve a two-second handshake either, but it
            brackets one to the right minute, which is what makes the line
            correlate with the rest of the log. Going slower trades that away;
            going faster costs only console bandwidth.

    config APRS_HEAP_REPORT_PER_HEAP
        bool "Add the per-heap breakdown to each heap line"
        depends on APRS_HEAP_REPORT
        default n
        help
            Follow each heap line with the table the heap component itself
            prints: one row per registered heap, carrying that heap's start
            address, size, current free, largest free block and all-time
            minimum free.

            This is the only view that separates the two things the summary
            line cannot tell apart. An ESP32 without PSRAM has several
            non-contiguous DRAM regions, each registered as a heap of its own,
            and the summary's minimum figure adds up each region's own
            watermark taken at its own worst instant. A small region driven to
            near zero once, hours before the main pool dipped, therefore
            produces the same summary as every region running out at the same
            moment - and the two call for entirely different work. The
            breakdown shows which region actually ran dry.

            Off by default because it is several lines per period rather than
            one. Turn it on for the run that answers the question, then off
            again once the answer is on record.

    config APRS_HEAP_REPORT_TASKS
        bool "Add the per-task usage table to each heap line"
        depends on APRS_HEAP_REPORT && HEAP_TASK_TRACKING
        default y
        help
            Follow each heap line with the per-task overview table that the
            heap component builds when task tracking is enabled, attributing
            the memory currently held to the task that allocated it.

            This is the tool that turns "the free heap keeps falling" into a
            task name in a single dump. It is only offered when
            CONFIG_HEAP_TASK_TRACKING is selected, which is deliberately not
            the default: tracking costs RAM per live allocation and slows
            every allocation and free down, so it belongs in a diagnostic
            build and should be turned off again afterwards.

    config APRS_HEAP_BRACKET
        bool "Log the heap on both sides of every heavy allocation path"
        default n
        help
            Emit one line immediately before and one immediately after each
            passage known to take a large or long-lived bite out of the heap:
            the Telegram bring-up, the APRS-IS connect, the configuration
            load, the console mirror's ring, the WiFi scan, the OTA upload and
            each web form buffer.

            The periodic line above says when the heap moved. A bracket says
            what moved it, because its two figures are taken on either side of
            one named passage rather than at whatever instant the period came
            due. Together they turn "the largest free block drops at these
            moments" into "the drops are these calls".

            The lines carry the same two figures, in the same memory class, as
            the ones CONFIG_TELEGRAM_BOT_HEAP_BRACKET prints around each API
            request, so a bracket from either side can be read against the
            other. Enabling both covers the request path and everything around
            it.

            Off by default: it is two lines per event, and the web form
            buffers make a pair on every settings save. It is meant to be
            turned on for a diagnostic session and off again afterwards.

    config APRS_STACK_REPORT
        bool "Log the stack headroom of every task"
        default y
        help
            Emit one line per task, on a slow period, with that task's stack
            high-water mark - the least headroom it has had since it started.

            Task stacks are the largest single block of RAM this firmware
            reserves and the one nobody measures: every stack size in the
            project is a budget set with deliberate headroom, not a figure
            trimmed to what the task turned out to need. Without this line the
            only way a stack reports being too small is by overflowing, and
            there is no way at all to learn that one is far too large.

            Every task is listed, not just this firmware's own. The WiFi, lwIP
            and event tasks the IDF runs have stacks that come out of the same
            memory, and a budget that ignores them is not a budget.

            One FreeRTOS query per period, and a fixed array of roughly a
            kilobyte that the report fills each time. The array is static so
            that a diagnostic about memory pressure cannot itself fail under
            memory pressure, and so that the tick task's stack does not have to
            be sized for something that runs once an hour.

    config APRS_STACK_REPORT_PERIOD_S
        int "Seconds between stack headroom lines"
        depends on APRS_STACK_REPORT
        range 60 86400
        default 3600
        help
            A high-water mark only ever falls, so this is not a sampler and
            nothing is missed between lines: each one reports the worst the
            task has seen since it started, whenever it is printed. The period
            decides only how soon a new worst case reaches the log.

            An hour is long enough to be invisible in the console and short
            enough that a mark set overnight is timestamped near the traffic
            that set it.

    config APRS_HEAP_INTEGRITY_CHECK
        bool "Run a periodic heap integrity check"
        default n
        help
            Sweep every heap on a slow timer and log the result, so that
            corruption is ruled out rather than chased as a leak. Corrupted
            allocator structures present as inexplicable heap behaviour: free
            counts that move without an obvious owner, allocations that fail
            with memory apparently available, crashes far from the code that
            caused them.

            The sweep holds each heap's lock while it walks it, so every other
            task blocks if it tries to allocate meanwhile. That is why this is
            off by default and on a slow timer when on.

            What the sweep can see depends on the heap corruption detection
            level. With the default (no poisoning) it only checks the
            allocator's own structures; select "Light impact" or
            "Comprehensive" under "Heap memory debugging" to also verify the
            canary bytes around every allocated block.

    config APRS_HEAP_INTEGRITY_PERIOD_S
        int "Seconds between heap integrity checks"
        depends on APRS_HEAP_INTEGRITY_CHECK
        range 10 3600
        default 300
        help
            The sweep is long enough to be worth spacing out. Five minutes
            still brackets corruption to a window small enough to correlate
            with the rest of the log, at a cost the station does not feel.

endmenu
